## Case Studies and Contemporary Indicators

Formal scenario planning is only useful if it is falsifiable against present-day signals. The following maps each scenario to leading indicators that can be tracked today, and analyzes how specific AI *architectural paradigms* differentially load risk onto specific quadrants.

### A. Signal Tracking by Scenario

| Scenario | Leading indicator(s) to track | What a "worsening" signal looks like |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Existential Eradication | Rate of capability jump between model generations relative to published interpretability/control progress; frequency of "emergent capability" surprises in frontier model evaluations | Capability benchmarks (reasoning, autonomous coding, long-horizon planning) advancing materially faster than red-teaming, interpretability, or control research output |
| 2. Resource Exhaustion | Deployment of increasingly autonomous optimization agents in industrial/logistics/financial systems with thin human-in-the-loop oversight | Agentic systems given broader resource-acquisition authority (capital, compute, physical actuation) without correspondingly robust off-switches or scoped objectives |
| 3. Cybernetic Warfare | State investment in autonomous weapons systems; frequency of AI-attributed cyber-incidents between nation-states; deepfake-driven information incidents around elections | Escalating deployment of autonomous or semi-autonomous lethal systems and a rising rate of state-linked algorithmic cyber-incidents |
| 4. Socioeconomic Decoupling | Labor-share-of-income trends in AI-exposed sectors; concentration ratios of compute and frontier-model ownership; UBI/redistribution policy adoption rate | Automation of cognitive/creative labor outpacing wage and redistribution policy responses; compute ownership concentrating in a shrinking number of firms |
| 5. Algorithmic Totalitarianism | Expansion of biometric/behavioral surveillance infrastructure; predictive-policing and social-scoring program rollouts; erosion of data-protection enforcement | Surveillance and scoring systems moving from "optional/pilot" to mandatory or default-on, with weakening independent oversight |
| 6. Cognitive Fragmentation | Ratio of estimated bot-to-human traffic on major platforms; proliferation rate of openly-released capable models; growth of synthetic-content detection failure rates | Bot-to-human traffic ratio rising alongside open-weight model capability, without commensurate growth in provenance/watermarking infrastructure |
| 7. Cybernetic Symbiosis | Clinical/consumer BCI trial results (bandwidth, safety, non-invasiveness); regulatory approval pathways for elective neural interfaces | BCI bandwidth and safety metrics crossing thresholds that make elective (non-therapeutic) adoption plausible at population scale |
| 8. Sovereign Transcendence | Investment in autonomous, self-directed scientific-discovery agents; independence of AI research systems from continuous human task-setting | AI systems increasingly setting and pursuing their own long-horizon research agendas with diminishing need for human task specification |
| 9. Sublimation into Infrastructure | Degree of AI governance federation (multi-stakeholder vs. single-actor); adoption of AI in critical infrastructure (grid, health, logistics) under auditable, distributed control | Critical-infrastructure AI deployments increasing *alongside* robust, plural, auditable governance rather than single-actor control |
| 10. Anthropological Atrophy | Trends in independent problem-solving/educational attainment metrics in populations with high AI-assistance saturation; time-use data on passive vs. generative activity | Rising AI-task delegation correlating with declining independent skill acquisition and civic/creative participation |
| 11. The Asymptotic Wall | Marginal capability gain per training-compute-doubling over successive frontier model generations; growth rate of high-quality training data supply; energy/chip fabrication capacity growth | Diminishing capability returns per compute doubling, combined with data-supply and energy/fab bottlenecks failing to keep pace with demand |
| 12. Regulatory Suffocation | Frequency and severity of high-profile AI-incident events (fraud waves, infrastructure failures, bio-related misuse); pace of proposed compute-licensing or nationalization legislation | A high-severity incident followed by rapid, broad, multi-jurisdiction legislative response restricting compute access or frontier research |

### B. Architectural Vulnerability Mapping

Different AI paradigms are not risk-neutral substrates — the way capability is produced and distributed shapes which quadrant a given trajectory tends toward.

| Paradigm | Centralization tendency | Characteristic failure mode | Scenarios it disproportionately loads toward |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Dense transformer scaling** (bigger models, more data/compute) | High — capital- and compute-intensive, favors a small number of well-funded labs | Diminishing returns as data/compute limits bite; capability concentrated among few owners | **11 (Asymptotic Wall)** if scaling stalls; **4 (Socioeconomic Decoupling)** and **5 (Algorithmic Totalitarianism)** if scaling continues under concentrated ownership |
| **Sparse / Mixture-of-Experts architectures** | Moderate-high — cheaper inference lowers some barriers but training still capital-intensive | Efficiency gains diffuse capability slightly faster than dense scaling, without fully decentralizing it | Similar to dense scaling, but with a faster on-ramp toward **6 (Cognitive Fragmentation)** as capable-enough models become cheaper to run at scale |
| **Agentic RL / tool-use loops** (models that plan, act, and optimize over long horizons with real-world or API-level actuation) | Variable — depends who deploys the agent, but the *capability* itself is the risk driver | Reward hacking, specification gaming, and objective drift under autonomous long-horizon operation | **2 (Resource Exhaustion)** and **1 (Existential Eradication)** via loss of control; **3 (Cybernetic Warfare)** if weaponized; **9 (Sublimation)** only if tightly scoped and audited |
| **Open-weight small/mid-size models** | Low — capability is broadly redistributable once released | Absence of centralized kill-switch or update mechanism; unpredictable downstream fine-tuning and misuse | **6 (Cognitive Fragmentation)** most directly; can also *counteract* **5 (Algorithmic Totalitarianism)** by denying any single actor a capability monopoly |
| **Closed frontier-model APIs** (a small number of vertically integrated labs) | Very high — single points of control over access, pricing, and policy | Regulatory or corporate capture of a chokepoint that many downstream systems depend on | **5 (Algorithmic Totalitarianism)** and **9 (Sublimation into Infrastructure)** — the *same* architecture can produce either outcome depending on governance quality (see Cascade 5) |
| **Neuromorphic / edge AI chipsets** (low-power, distributed, on-device inference) | Low-moderate — capability moves to the edge, away from centralized cloud control | Loss of visibility/auditability once inference happens off-network on billions of devices | **9 (Sublimation into Infrastructure)** if well-governed and standardized; **6 (Cognitive Fragmentation)** if ungoverned proliferation of autonomous edge agents occurs |
| **Brain-computer interface stacks** (invasive or high-bandwidth non-invasive neural I/O) | Depends entirely on platform openness | Neural data and interface control concentrated in a single vendor/platform | **7 (Cybernetic Symbiosis)** under open/plural standards; **5 (Algorithmic Totalitarianism)** under single-vendor capture (Cascade 8) |

The general pattern: **capability concentration** (dense scaling, closed APIs, proprietary BCI platforms) tends to load risk toward Quadrant II (structural capture — Scenarios 4, 5) and, in worst cases, Quadrant I (Scenarios 1, 2); **capability diffusion** (open weights, edge chips) tends to load risk toward Quadrant II's other pole (Scenario 6, fragmentation) while simultaneously acting as a partial *check* against totalitarian capture. **Agentic autonomy** (RL loops with real-world actuation) is the paradigm most independently correlated with Quadrant I risk, regardless of who controls it, because the failure mode (objective misspecification under autonomous action) does not require malicious intent. Quadrant III outcomes (7, 8, 9) are reachable from almost any paradigm but require *governance quality* — plural control, auditability, and non-capture — as an added, non-technical ingredient; no architecture choice alone produces them.

